WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.708
Everything NOSHASHI reads sits on a few basic ideas about the XRP Ledger.

2
00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:07.697
Here they are, one at a time.

3
00:00:09.397 --> 00:00:13.152
There is no miner and no single company deciding what is true.

4
00:00:13.602 --> 00:00:19.426
Instead, independent servers called validators vote on which transactions go into the next ledger.

5
00:00:19.876 --> 00:00:22.832
Each server trusts a list of validators, its U N L.

6
00:00:22.832 --> 00:00:25.615
When at least eighty percent of that list agree,

7
00:00:26.065 --> 00:00:27.517
the ledger is validated.

8
00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:29.755
It is final, and it can never change.

9
00:00:30.205 --> 00:00:35.463
A new ledger closes every three to five seconds, each with a higher ledger index.

10
00:00:35.463 --> 00:00:39.293
That index is how NOSHASHI stamps the exact moment it read.

11
00:00:40.993 --> 00:00:44.961
An account is a place on the ledger that holds XRP and tokens.

12
00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:49.761
Its address starts with the letter r, like Bitstamp's issuing account here.

13
00:00:50.211 --> 00:00:52.323
Control comes from a secret key.

14
00:00:52.323 --> 00:00:57.669
Whoever holds the key owns the account, which is why NOSHASHI never asks for one.

15
00:00:57.669 --> 00:00:59.385
It only reads public data.

16
00:00:59.835 --> 00:01:11.835
Every account also keeps some XRP locked as a reserve: currently one XRP to exist, plus zero point two XRP for each object it owns, such as a trust line or an offer.

17
00:01:11.835 --> 00:01:15.835
So a balance is not the same as what can actually move.

18
00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:23.627
Every change is a transaction: a payment, an offer, a new trust line, a changed setting.

19
00:01:23.627 --> 00:01:28.820
Each pays a tiny fee in XRP, which is destroyed rather than paid to anyone.

20
00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.446
A result starting with tes means success.

21
00:01:31.896 --> 00:01:36.973
A result starting with tec means it failed, but it was still recorded, and the fee is gone.

22
00:01:37.423 --> 00:01:40.950
The answer the server gives at first is only tentative.

23
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:48.517
The final result is fixed in the transaction's metadata once the ledger is validated, and that is what NOSHASHI reads.

24
00:01:50.217 --> 00:01:52.027
Any account can issue a token.

25
00:01:52.027 --> 00:01:57.577
To hold one, you open a trust line to its issuer, which records your limit and your balance.

26
00:01:58.027 --> 00:02:01.898
Anyone can use the code U S D, so the name proves nothing.

27
00:02:01.898 --> 00:02:06.837
Always ask who issued it: the issuer address is the token's real identity.

28
00:02:07.287 --> 00:02:12.991
The ledger reports each issuer's obligations, the total of its tokens that others hold.

29
00:02:12.991 --> 00:02:15.351
That is the real circulating supply.

30
00:02:17.051 --> 00:02:21.119
New features, like clawback or credentials, arrive as amendments.

31
00:02:21.119 --> 00:02:27.377
An amendment needs at least eighty percent of validators to support it, continuously, for two weeks.

32
00:02:27.827 --> 00:02:30.228
Then it switches on for everyone at once.

33
00:02:30.228 --> 00:02:34.269
NOSHASHI's Domain Grid shows which amendments are enabled on mainnet.
